  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 755 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nA large upper level trough will develop across the eastern\nUnited States over the next several days, with associated high \npressure bringing much cooler weather to the central and eastern\nUSA. However, western Kansas will be on the western periphery \nof the cool air mass, with surface high pressure settling across\nthe central plains Friday morning. The coolest readings will be\nacross central Kansas, with milder temperatures across far \nsouthwest Kansas. Temperatures may fall to near or below \nfreezing early Friday; but the long awaited hard freeze is \nhighly unlikely except for perhaps Larned and Hays where upper \n20s are likely. The latest, first hard freeze at Dodge City was\non 22 November 1934; and this may be challenged this fall. \nAfternoon temperatures will be progressively cooler into \nThursday and Friday in the wake of the surface high pressure as \nsoutheasterly upslope flow has a cooling effect and recirculates\ncool air. Expect highs in the 50s in central Kansas, with near \n60 in the southwest corner.\n\nAn intense upper level storm system west of the Aleutians will\nweaken as it progresses southeastward into the Desert Southwest\nby Friday and Saturday. Over the past few days the various\nensemble systems have been in disagreement with the intensity \nof this system. More specifically, some solutions continue to \nclose the system off and pass it across the southern plains. \nThis would be a wet solution for the southern half of western \nKansas for Saturday night and Sunday. Other solutions are \nslower to close the system off or have the system farther north \nor south, which would impact who gets rain and how much. The \ntrack and intensity of this system depends on the speed of the \nnext system moving off the coast of Japan near 145 west \nlongitude. If this trailing system is faster, then it would push\nthe lead system along faster, resulting in less rain across \nsouthwest Kansas. Any rain into an already cool air mass, along \nwith easterly upslope flow and solid cloud cover, would result \nin cool highs in the 40s to lower 50s for Sunday. Concerning \nrain amounts, all of the surface based instability and most of \nthe elevated CAPE will stay south of Kansas, along with any \nchance for severe weather. However, an extended period of light \nrain could result in significant rain amounts given how \nclimatologically dry we are this time of year. The various \nensemble means indicate the potential of .10\" to .50\", with the \nhighest amounts along the Oklahoma state line from Meade to \nColdwater to Medicine Lodge, and much less along I-70. The grand\nensemble shows 60% chances for at least .25\" along the Oklahoma\nstate line but only 30-40% chances for at least .5\" there. \nSignificant rain chances are much lower along I-70 per grand \nensemble.\n\nThe trailing system (mentioned above) is expected to approach\nthe high plains by the middle of next week. Whether this system\nbrings significant rain to southwest Kansas depends on the\nspeed and track, which are highly uncertain.",
